successfully completed "Basic System Logs", a practical lab focused on understanding, navigating, and analyzing Linux system log files. This lab strengthened my ability to monitor system health, troubleshoot issues, and observe real-time system behavior.
System logs are critical for diagnosing problems, tracking events, and maintaining the overall stability and security of a Linux environment. This lab gave me hands-on experience working with the standard logging infrastructure.
Key Skills Acquired
1. Navigating System Log Directory
Bashcd /var/log
ls -lh

Located and explored the /var/log directory, the central repository for system and application logs.
Used ls -lh to view files with human-readable sizes and permissions.

2. Viewing and Analyzing Logs
Bashless syslog        # or less messages
grep "error" syslog

Accessed main system log files (syslog or messages depending on the distribution).
Efficiently searched for specific keywords like "error" using grep.

3. Real-time Log Monitoring
Bashtail -f syslog     # or tail -f messages

Used tail -f to monitor logs in real-time as new entries are written.
Learned how to interrupt monitoring with Ctrl + C.

Concepts Mastered

Structure and purpose of the /var/log directory
Difference between common log files (syslog, messages, auth.log, kern.log, etc.)
Effective use of less, grep, and tail for log analysis
Real-time system observation techniques
Importance of logs in troubleshooting and proactive system maintenance

